WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 270: Clara Barton: The Angel of the Battlefield, Antietam, and the American Red Cross

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.600
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:22.000
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series honors the fearless pioneer of frontline humanitarian medicine, the tiny Massachusetts schoolteacher who carried bandages into the cannon smoke and founded the American Red Cross.

4
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.280
[Rebecca] Here is George with the life of Clara Barton.

5
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:40.720
[George] Born on Christmas Day 1821 in the farming town of North Oxford, Massachusetts, Clarissa Harlow Barton was an intensely shy, sensitive child who was terrified of strangers and loud voices.

6
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:46.720
[George] Yet beneath her quiet exterior lay a profound capacity for physical endurance and medical nursing.

7
00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:55.280
[George] When she was 11 years old, her older brother David fell from the ridgepole of a barn, suffering severe head trauma and internal injuries.

8
00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:07.440
[George] For two grueling years, young Clara remained at his bedside around the clock, administering medications, applying leeches, and nursing him through delirium until he made a miraculous recovery.

9
00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:13.600
[George] Recognizing her intelligence, her parents encouraged her to become a schoolteacher at age 17.

10
00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:16.800
[George] Barton possessed a magnetic pedagogical gift.

11
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:23.760
[George] She refused to use corporal punishment, earning the obedience of rowdy boys by playing sports with them in the yard.

12
00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:34.800
[George] In 1852, moving to Bordentown, New Jersey, Barton founded the town's first free public school, expanding enrollment from six pupils to over 600.

13
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:55.680
[George] But when the town school board appointed a male principal at double her salary, asserting that a woman was unfit to administer a large institution, Barton resigned in disgust, suffering a severe nervous collapse and moving to Washington, D.C., in 1854 to become the first female recording clerk in the United States Patent Office.

14
00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:12.840
[George] Born on Christmas Day 1821 in North Oxford, Massachusetts, Clarissa Harlow Barton was intensely timid as a child, finding comfort in caring for sick pets and nursing her older brother David through a two-year recovery following a catastrophic barn roof fall.

15
00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:24.040
[George] Becoming a rural schoolteacher at age 17, she established New Jersey's first free public school in Bordentown, expanding enrollment from six pupils to over 600.

16
00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:37.800
[George] When the town board hired a male principal at twice her salary to oversee the school she had built, Barton resigned in principled protest, moving to Washington, D.C., to work as a clerk in the United States Patent Office.

17
00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:55.720
[George] On April 19, 1861, one week after the Confederate bombardment of Fort Sumter ignited the Civil War, the 6th Massachusetts militia was mobbed by pro-Confederate rioters as they marched between train stations in Baltimore, resulting in four dead soldiers and dozens of wounded.

18
00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:04.120
[George] When the battered regiment arrived in Washington and was quartered in the unfinished Senate chamber of the Capitol, Clara Barton rushed to their aid.

19
00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:10.120
[George] She discovered that many of the wounded boys were former students and childhood neighbors from Massachusetts.

20
00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:21.800
[George] Turning her rented apartment on 7th Street into a bustling supply depot.

21
00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:32.280
[George] As the bloody reality of the war unfolded at the first Battle of Bull Run in July 1861, Barton was appalled by the complete failure of Army medical logistics.

22
00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:42.600
[George] Wounded soldiers lay for days in the woods without water, food or medical attention, dying of dehydration and maggots before reaching crude Washington hospitals.

23
00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:47.840
[George] Barton realized that hospital care was useless if men bled to death on the field.

24
00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.240
[George] Supplies had to reach the frontlines.

25
00:03:50.240 --> 00:04:06.560
[George] In April 1861, when the 6th Massachusetts Regiment was assaulted by pro-Confederate rioters in Baltimore and arrived wounded at the Washington Railroad Terminal, Barton rushed to the Senate chamber with towels, clean bandages and bowls of hot soup.

26
00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:25.840
[George] Recognizing that government supply lines repeatedly collapsed during major battles, leaving wounded men to starve and bleed to death on raw fields, Barton placed advertisements in New England newspapers for bandages, canned food and lanterns, converting her private apartment into a massive relief depot.

27
00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:34.560
[George] For a year, army generals and conservative bureaucrats refused to grant a solitary woman permission to enter active military combat zones.

28
00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:48.960
[George] But in August 1862, through relentless lobbying of Quartermaster General Montgomery Mages and assistant Quartermaster General Daniel Rucker, 40-year-old Clara Barton was granted official passes to travel with the Army.

29
00:04:48.960 --> 00:05:06.240
[George] On the bloodiest day in American history, September 17, 1862, at the Battle of Antietam in Maryland, Barton drove her mule-drawn wagon through cannon fire, arriving at a makeshift field hospital set up in a farmhouse behind the cornfield at 10 o'clock in the morning.

30
00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:12.480
[George] Army surgeon James Dunn was down to his last scrap of bandage and operating in near-total darkness.

31
00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:17.760
[George] Barton unloaded boxes of lint, bandages, chloroform, and lanterns.

32
00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:29.920
[George] As she knelt in the dirt giving a drink of water to a wounded soldier, a Confederate mini ball tore through the sleeve of her dress and struck the soldier directly in the chest, killing him instantly in her arms.

33
00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:42.280
[George] Never repairing the torn sleeve, Barton washed her face in muddy creek water and continued assisting surgeons with amputations until nightfall, using corn husks for bandages when supplies ran out.

34
00:05:42.280 --> 00:05:46.600
[George] Surgeon Dunn rode in awe, if heaven ever sent an angel to earth.

35
00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:47.880
[George] She was one.

36
00:05:47.880 --> 00:06:02.120
[George] At the bloody sunken road of Antietam on September 17, 1862, Barton drove her supply wagons directly onto the active battlefield, unloading cornmeal, clean bandages, and casks of brandy for the wounded.

37
00:06:02.120 --> 00:06:13.400
[George] Kneeling in the mud to offer a cup of water to a dying Union soldier, a Confederate bullet ripped through the sleeve of her dress, striking the wounded private in the chest and killing him instantly.

38
00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:21.480
[George] Unshaken by the close brush with death, Barton wrung out her torn sleeve and continued tending to the fallen under artillery fire.

39
00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:26.440
[George] Barton's frontline bravery became legendary throughout the Army of the Potomac.

40
00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:37.880
[George] At the Battle of Fredericksburg in December 1862, while crossing the pontoon bridge under heavy Confederate sniper fire, a piece of artillery shrapnel tore away part of her skirt.

41
00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:44.600
[George] Inside the Lacey House Hospital, she treated hundreds of shattered soldiers lying on blood-soaked floors.

42
00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:58.280
[George] In 1863, she traveled to the siege of Morris Island in South Carolina, nursing the wounded black soldiers of the famous 54th Massachusetts Infantry following their suicidal assault on Fort Wagner.

43
00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:17.200
[George] During General Grant's brutal overland campaign in 1864, Barton rode through dense forest fires at the Battle of the Wilderness and the slaughterhouse of Cold Harbor, feeding thousands of wounded men hot beef tea, washing severed limbs, and writing down final letters home for dying farm boys.

44
00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:31.440
[George] Possessing a commanding executive presence, Barton cowed lazy army wagon drivers, stared down drunken officers, and ensured that medical relief reached the dying regardless of whether they wore Union blue or Confederate gray.

45
00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:33.040
[George] Following Robert E.

46
00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:47.200
[George] Lee's surrender at Appomattox, President Abraham Lincoln authorized Barton to establish the missing soldier's office in Washington, D.C., to answer the desperate letters of grieving mothers and wives searching for vanished soldiers.

47
00:07:47.200 --> 00:08:09.120
[George] Working alongside former Union prisoner Dorence Atwater, who had secretly copied the burial records of 13,000 Union soldiers who died in the horrific Confederate stockade at Andersonville, Georgia, Barton led an expedition to mark and dedicate each soldier's grave, transforming a barren clearing of mass graves into Andersonville National Cemetery.

48
00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:16.400
[George] When the war ended in April 1865, Barton recognized that a horrific humanitarian crisis remained.

49
00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:28.080
[George] Over 300,000 Union soldiers were missing, their families in agonizing uncertainty over whether their sons and husbands were alive, imprisoned, or buried in unmarked graves.

50
00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:38.080
[George] With the personal blessing of President Abraham Lincoln, Barton established the office of correspondence with the friends of the missing men of the United States Army in Washington.

51
00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:47.440
[George] Working out of her boarding house, Barton and her assistants answered 63,000 desperate letters from families, cross-referencing military roles.

52
00:08:47.440 --> 00:09:02.680
[George] In the summer of 1865, collaborating with former Union prisoner Dorence Atwater, who had secretly copied the death registers at the notorious Confederate Prisoner of War camp at Andersonville, Georgia, Barton traveled to the disease-ridden prison site.

53
00:09:02.920 --> 00:09:16.680
[George] Leading an expedition, she identified and properly marked the graves of nearly 13,000 Union soldiers who had perished from starvation and dysentery, raising the stars and stripes over the Andersonville National Cemetery.

54
00:09:16.680 --> 00:09:26.120
[George] Across four years, Barton's office identified and accounted for 22,000 missing soldiers, bringing peace to grieving families across the nation.

55
00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:32.840
[George] Traveling to Switzerland for medical rest following years of wartime exhaustion, Barton was introduced to Dr.

56
00:09:32.920 --> 00:09:41.240
[George] Louis Appea and the founders of the International Committee of the Red Cross, who introduced her to the 1864 Geneva Convention.

57
00:09:41.240 --> 00:09:48.440
[George] Stunned that the United States had refused to sign the treaty due to traditional isolationism and fear of foreign entanglements.

58
00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:07.240
[George] Exhausted by her war work, Barton suffered a physical collapse and traveled to Europe in 1869 to rest.

59
00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:19.240
[George] But in Geneva, Switzerland, she was visited by representatives of the International Committee of the Red Cross, founded by Swiss humanitarian Henry Dunant following the bloody battle of Salferino.

60
00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:31.880
[George] The Red Cross operated under the 1864 Geneva Convention, which established the neutral protection of wounded soldiers and medical personnel on the battlefield under the emblem of a Red Cross on a white field.

61
00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:39.480
[George] The United States was the only major civilized nation that had refused to ratify the treaty, fearing foreign entanglements.

62
00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:55.840
[George] When the Franco-Prussian War erupted in 1870, Barton immediately joined the International Red Cross on the frontlines, entering besieged Strasbourg and Paris to organize relief for starving civilians, distributing food and clothing to thousands of war refugees.

63
00:10:55.840 --> 00:11:10.320
[George] Decorated with the Iron Cross by Kaiser Wilhelm I of Germany, Barton returned to America with a burning, holy mission, to compel the United States government to ratify the Geneva Convention and establish the American Red Cross.

64
00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:25.120
[George] Founding the American Red Cross in Washington, D.C., on May 21, 1881, Barton served as its first president for 23 years, establishing a national network of volunteers dedicated to emergency relief.

65
00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:44.640
[George] Barton authored the historic American Amendment to the International Red Cross Constitution, expanding the organization's charter beyond wartime combat to encompass peacetime civilian disasters, including devastating floods, hurricanes, forest fires, yellow fever epidemics, and famines.

66
00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:54.720
[George] For nearly a decade, Barton lobbied four consecutive presidents, Grant, Hayes, Garfield and Arthur, against stubborn bureaucratic isolationism.

67
00:11:54.720 --> 00:12:03.040
[George] In May 1881, she incorporated the American Red Cross in Washington, and in March 1882, President Chester A.

68
00:12:03.280 --> 00:12:09.360
[George] Arthur finally signed the treaty, making the United States a signatory to the Geneva Convention.

69
00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:24.720
[George] But Barton introduced a revolutionary innovation known as the American Amendment, expanding the Red Cross mandate beyond wartime military care to include peacetime humanitarian relief during natural disasters, floods, famines, and epidemics.

70
00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:26.800
[George] For the next 23 years.

71
00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:49.480
[George] Until age 83, Barton personally directed relief operations, aiding victims of the 1889 Johnstown flood, rushing food to the Sea Islands Hurricane of 1893, providing famine relief to Armenia and Russia, nursing soldiers during the Spanish American War in Cuba, and directing relief for the 1900 Galveston hurricane.

72
00:12:49.480 --> 00:12:56.520
[George] Clara Barton passed away peacefully at her Glen Echo, Maryland, home in April 1912 at age 90.

73
00:12:56.520 --> 00:13:07.800
[George] Her legacy is woven into the conscience of modern humanity, the timid girl who conquered fear to bring mercy to the battlefield, proving that compassion is the highest form of human courage.

74
00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:26.040
[George] At the age of 77, during the Spanish-American War in 1898, Barton sailed aboard the steamship state of Texas to Cuba, riding into the fever-ridden trenches of Sibony to personally distribute food and surgical supplies to wounded American soldiers and starving Cuban refugees.

75
00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:38.600
[George] Her historic Victorian home and warehouse at Glen Echo, Maryland, with its deep cedar closets stocked with emergency stretchers and medical kits, stood as a living symbol of humanitarian preparedness.

76
00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:47.400
[George] Clara Barton's life proved that selfless courage and compassionate organization could conquer the darkest horrors of war and disaster.

77
00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:06.840
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

